Steps for creation and configuration of the IPsec tunnel

1) we update our repositories: sudo apt update
Then we install StrongSwan using the commands: sudo apt install strongswan strongswan-starter libcharon-extra-plugins libcharon-extauth-plugins -y
The "libcharon-extra-plugins" adds support for extra auth methods and cipher suites where the "libcharon-extauth-plugins" specifically adds EAP auth support (needed for username/password login)

2) creation of dummy interface 

# load the dummy kernel module
sudo modprobe dummy

# creation of  dummy interface
sudo ip link add dummy0 type dummy
sudo ip addr add 10.X.X.1/24 dev dummy0
sudo ip link set dummy0 up

# we make it persistent across reboots by creating a systemd-networkd config and apply some commands:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/network/10-dummy0.netdev

[NetDev]
Name=dummy0
Kind=dummy

sudo nano /etc/systemd/network/20-dummy0.network
[Match]
Name=dummy0

[Network]
Address=10.X.X.1/24

# enable the service 
sudo systemctl enable --now systemd-networkd
sudo systemctl restart systemd-networkd

3) Generation of PKI (self-signed CA and self-sgned cert)
This is where we build our own mini Certificate Authority for the VPN. IPsec with IKEv2 relies on certificates to prove identity.

First we need to create directories for our CA, certs, and private keys and then change the permissions so we can have only us permissions and no one else: mkdir -p ~/pki/{ca,certs,private} and then we type chmod 700 ~/pki

# We generate a private key for the CA, then self-sign a certificate for it. This CA is the "root of trust", which means that both sides will trust anything signed by it.

To generate the CA key and self-signed cert we use the commands: openssl genrsa -out ~/pki/private/ca-key.pem 4096
openssl req -new -x509 -days 3650 \
    -key ~/pki/private/ca-key.pem \
    -out ~/pki/ca/ca-cert.pem \
    -subj "/CN=VPN-Lab-CA"

# We generate a separate key for the server, a certificate signing request for the server and an extension file. Then the CA sign a certificate for the server. 
# A CSR contains public key info and identity details that a CA signs.

To generate server key and cert that is signed by CA: openssl genrsa -out ~/pki/private/server-key.pem 4096
openssl req -new \
    -key ~/pki/private/server-key.pem \
    -out ~/pki/private/server.csr \
    -subj "/CN=<SERVER_IP_OR_HOSTNAME>"

# Server CSR (Certificate Signing Request)
openssl req -new \
    -key ~/pki/private/server-key.pem \
    -out ~/pki/private/server.csr \
    -subj "/CN=<SERVER_IP_OR_HOSTNAME>"

We create a v3 extension file for the server cert that containing X.509 v3 extensions required for secure IPsec/IKEv2 connections:. We need to explicitly define the extensions in a file:

cat > ~/pki/server.cnf << EOF
[req]
default_bits = 4096
prompt = no
distinguished_name = dn
req_extensions = v3_req

[dn]
CN = 192.168.X.X

[v3_req]
subjectAltName = IP:192.168.X.X
extendedKeyUsage = serverAuth
keyUsage = digitalSignature, keyEncipherment
EOF

# digitalSignature: allows the key to generate signatures (used during IKE negotiation to prove identity)
# keyEncipherment: allows the key to encrypt symmetric session keys (used during transport key exchanges)
# The --san (Subject Alternative Name) field is critical, it must match the IP/hostname the client connects to, otherwise the handshake will fail. 
# The serverAuth flag tells the certificate it's allowed to authenticate a server.

Sign the server cert with the CA:

openssl x509 -req -days 1825 \ -> invokes OpenSSL's X.509 certificate handling utility, which is used here to transform and sign certificate requests and tells OpenSSL that the input file is a CSR rather than an existing certificate
    -in ~/pki/private/server.csr \
    -CA ~/pki/ca/ca-cert.pem \
    -CAkey ~/pki/private/ca-key.pem \
    -CAcreateserial \ -> instructs OpenSSL to create a serial number file (ca-cert.srl) if it doesn't already exist. Every certificate issued by a CA must have a unique serial number
    -out ~/pki/certs/server-cert.pem \
    -extensions v3_req \ -> tells OpenSSL which section header inside your configuration file to pull extensions from (in this case, the [v3_req] block).
    -extfile ~/pki/server-ext.cnf -> passes the configuration file containing your extra X.509 v3 extension flags (Subject Alternative Name, Extended Key Usage, Key Usage).

We verify that the cert looks correct: openssl x509 -in ~/pki/certs/server-cert.pem -text -noout | grep -A2 "Subject Alternative\|Extended Key" -> we should see our IP/hostname in the SAN and TLS Web Server Authentication in the EKU.
  
4) StrongSwan reads from specific directories under /etc/ipsec.d/

To install the Certs into StrongSwan we need to copy and paste them on specific folder: sudo cp ~/pki/ca/ca-cert.pem /etc/ipsec.d/cacerts/ 
sudo cp ~/pki/certs/server-cert.pem /etc/ipsec.d/certs/
sudo cp ~/pki/private/server-key.pem /etc/ipsec.d/private/ 

5) Tunnel configuration

We configure the file /etc/ipsec.conf using the nano text editor: sudo nano /etc/ipsec.conf

config setup
    charondebug="ike 1, knl 1, cfg 0"
    uniqueids=no -> allows multiple connections from the same identity

conn ikev2-vpn -> defines one tunnel profile
    auto=add
    compress=no
    type=tunnel
    keyexchange=ikev2 -> we force to use IKEv2
    fragmentation=yes
    forceencaps=yes -> forces UDP encapsulation (NAT-T on port 4500), important when there's NAT between the peers, I enable it for future implementation

    # Encryption/integrity proposals -> the cipher proposals. IKE is for the control channel (key exchange), ESP is for the actual data tunnel. The exclamation mark at the end means "only these, no fallback"
    ike=aes256-sha256-modp2048,aes128-sha256-modp2048!
    esp=aes256-sha256,aes128-sha256!

    dpdaction=restart
    dpddelay=30s
    dpdtimeout=120s
    rekey=yes

    # Ubuntu-server side (local)
    left=192.168.X.X -> IP of the WAN interface
    leftid=192.168.X.X
    leftcert=server-cert.pem
    leftsendcert=always
    leftsubnet=10.X.X.0/24

    # pfsense side (remote)
    right=192.168.X.X -> WAN IP
    rightid=192.168.X.X
    rightcert=pfsense-cert.pem
    rightsubnet=192.168.X.0/24

6) we configure the /etc/ipsec.secrets using again the nano text editor: sudo nano /etc/ipsec.secrets
# RSA private key for server auth
: RSA "server-key.pem" -> tells StrongSwan to use this private key to prove the server's identity during the IKE handshake
  
# VPN user credentials (EAP)
vpnuser : EAP "type whatever password you want" -> the username/password pair that the client will authenticate with

7) we enable IP Forwarding by editing the sysctl file: sudo nano /etc/sysctl.conf

# By default, Linux drops packets that aren't destined for itself. Since the VPN server needs to forward traffic from VPN clients to the rest of the network (and back), we need to enable this at the kernel level.

Uncomment or add:

net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 -> allows the kernel to route packets between interfaces
net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_redirects = 0 -> disables ICMP redirects from other routers or hosts on the network, which can interfere with VPN routing
net.ipv4.conf.all.send_redirects = 0 -> -> stops the server from emitting outgoing ICMP Redirect messages to connected clients

We apply them using the command: sudo sysctl -p
  
8) we configure UFW / iptables to open the ports that used for the IPsec

If using UFW:

sudo ufw allow 500/udp      # IKE
sudo ufw allow 4500/udp     # NAT-T
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH

Also we add NAT rules before the *filter block in /etc/ufw/before.rules:

*nat
-A POSTROUTING -s 10.X.X.X/X -o <WAN_INTERFACE> -m policy --pol ipsec --dir out -j ACCEPT -> tells iptables to bypass NAT for traffic that matches an active IPsec policy. This ensures encrypted IPsec tunnel traffic (like site-to-site traffic) retains its original IP headers so the remote endpoint can properly match and decrypt it.
-A POSTROUTING -s 10.X.X.X/X -o <WAN_INTERFACE> -j MASQUERADE -> translates the private VPN IP addresses (10.X.X.X/X) into the server’s public WAN IP for all other outgoing traffic. This allows roadwarrior VPN clients to browse the public internet through your server.
COMMIT

*mangle
-A FORWARD --match policy --pol ipsec --dir in -s 10.X.X.X/X -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN -m tcpmss --mss 1361:1536 -j TCPMSS --set-mss 1360 -> this rule inspects outgoing TCP SYN packets and forcibly clamps the Maximum Segment Size (MSS) down to 1360 bytes. This leaves enough headroom for IPsec headers, preventing web pages from hanging or failing to load.
COMMIT

Replace <WAN_INTERFACE> with your actual interface (e.g. ens33, eth0).

Also in /etc/default/ufw set:

DEFAULT_FORWARD_POLICY="ACCEPT" -> by default, UFW drops all forwarded traffic (packets passing through the server from one interface to another). Changing this to ACCEPT tells UFW's filter table to allow the kernel to pass traffic between your virtual IPsec interfaces and your physical network interface.

Then we enable the rules: sudo ufw disable && sudo ufw enable

9) we start & enable StrongSwan using the commands: sudo systemctl enable --now strongswan-starter
To restart we use the command: sudo systemctl restart strongswan-starter and to verify the status we the command sudo systemctl status strongswan-starter

10) verification commands of IPsec tunnel
sudo ipsec status         # Check loaded connections
sudo ipsec statusall      # Full detail
